course: everyday
lesson: 404
title: Parents' rules for big games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Jayden wanted a 16 game for his tenth birthday, so he did what any serious person would do: he made a five-page presentation. Slide one: «Reasons». Slide two: «More reasons». Slide five was a picture of a sad puppy with the words «Please». His parents laughed so hard his dad had to sit down. They still said no to the 16 game — but they said yes to a 12 one, «because anyone who can make slides like that can make a good case».
explain: Ruby's parents did something clever: before deciding, they sat down and watched a long gameplay video of the game she wanted. Twenty minutes in, her mum said, «Right, the shooting's cartoony, but the chat with strangers is a no.» So Ruby got the game with voice chat switched off. Kai tried the classic: «Everyone in my class has it,» about an 18 game. His mum said, «Name three.» He named one. It turned out that boy had only watched his brother play it.
explain: Every family is different. Theo's parents allow 12 games at ten, but only if they play the first hour with him. Zara's family has a rule that any new game lives in the living room for its first month. Leo's dad lets him try an older game as long as he stops if he feels upset — and tells his dad. Noah's mum simply said, «Not until you're 16, and I will not change my mind,» and she meant it, so Noah stopped asking and saved his breath for something else.
explain: Sometimes parents don't agree with each other. Priya's mum thought a 12 game was fine; her dad wasn't sure. They argued about it at dinner, not angrily, just properly — and then agreed to watch it together first. Priya learned that grown-ups have to work out rules too. And when Mia asked, «Why can grown-ups play 18 games but I can't?» her mum explained that ratings are like film ratings: some things are fine for an adult brain that knows what's real, and too much for a younger one that is still learning.
explain: Ollie tried a shortcut: he secretly downloaded the free demo of an older game on the family console. Ten minutes later his mum's phone pinged — the console account sends her an email for every download. He lost the console for two weeks. Not for playing a demo, his mum said, but for sneaking. The lesson the whole class agreed on: arguing your case politely can work; sneaking almost never does.
fact: In the UK and Europe the PEGI age ratings are 3, 7, 12, 16 and 18.
fact: In the USA and Canada the ESRB ratings include E for Everyone, E10+, T for Teen and M for Mature (17 and over).
fact: In the UK it is against the law for a shop to sell a game rated 12, 16 or 18 to a child younger than that age.
fact: Consoles and game accounts have parental controls that can limit games by age and tell parents about downloads.
fact: Some games are fine for an age but have extras, such as chat with strangers, that parents may switch off.
fact: Watching gameplay videos or reading reviews is a good way for parents to decide about a game.
fact: Different families make different rules, and both are allowed to be right for their own children.
fact: «Everyone has it» is rarely true and is not a good reason on its own.
fact: Parents may disagree with each other and need time to agree on a rule.
fact: A calm, polite request with good reasons is more likely to be listened to than nagging or sneaking.
fact: Ratings exist because younger children are more easily upset and affected by violent or frightening content.
exercise: Why did Jayden's parents say yes to a 12 game? | His presentation was a good, polite case | He cried and sulked for a whole week until they gave in | Everyone in his class already had it | He saved up and found the money himself
exercise: How did Ruby's parents decide about the game? | They watched a gameplay video first | They asked the shop assistant to choose for them | They tossed a coin at the kitchen table | They let Ruby decide alone
exercise: What did Ruby's parents switch off? | Voice chat with strangers | The music and sound effects | The whole console at the wall | All the bright colours on the screen
exercise: Kai said «everyone in my class has it». What happened? | He could only name one, who hadn't even played it | His mum believed him and bought it straight away | The whole class wrote a letter to back him up | His teacher wrote a note
exercise: Is «everyone has it» a good reason on its own? | No, it's rarely true and not a reason | Yes, it's always the best reason you can give | Yes, as long as you say it three times | No, unless it's an 18 game
exercise: What is Theo's family's rule for 12 games? | A parent plays the first hour with him | He plays them only late at night, after bedtime | He can play any game alone in his room | He must be 12 first
exercise: Why might Zara's family keep new games in the living room? | So parents can see what the game is like | Because the TV is bigger and the sound is better there | Because games are banned upstairs | To stop Zara's little brother from playing it
exercise: What is Leo's deal with his dad? | He can play if he stops when he feels upset | He can play anything at all, as long as he's quiet | He can only play for an hour on Sundays | He must win every match
exercise: Noah's mum says «not until you're 16». What's the wise response? | Accept it and stop nagging | Ask every hour until she gives in | Play it at a friend's in secret | Sulk in his room for a whole year
exercise: What did Priya learn when her parents disagreed? | Grown-ups have to work out rules too | Her parents were about to get divorced over a game | Dad always wins the arguments at home | Rules are made up on the spot
exercise: Why can adults play 18 games when Mia can't? | Adults are less affected by violent and scary content | Adults are quicker and better at pressing the buttons | Adults paid for the console, so they choose | Adults never get scared
exercise: How did Ollie's mum find out about the demo? | The console emailed her about the download | Ollie's big sister told her at dinner | She saw the guilty look on his face | She checks the console's history every single night
exercise: Why did Ollie lose the console? | For sneaking, not for the demo itself | Because downloading demos is illegal | Because he lost the game and got angry | Because he played too well
exercise: Which is the best way to ask for a game? | Calmly, with good reasons, and accept the answer | Nag every day, morning and night, until they say yes | Tell them everyone else in class has it | Download it and say sorry later
exercise: What kind of courage does it take to accept a «no»? | Staying calm when you're disappointed | Sneaking it anyway and hoping nobody notices | Shouting louder than they do | Telling your friends your parents are mean
exercise: Can two families with different rules both be right? | Yes, each knows its own children | No, one of the two families must be wrong | No, only the strictest family is right | Yes, but only if they live far apart
test: «made a 5 page powerpoint for a game lol». Is that silly? | No, a polite case with reasons is a good way to ask | Yes, parents never bother to read them anyway | Yes, crying and begging works much better than slides | Only if it has no puppies
test: «my mum watched a 20 min video of the game before saying yes, so embarrassing». Was it a good idea? | Yes, it's a smart way to decide | No, she should just say yes | No, videos are always fake | Only if she plays it too
test: «EVERYONE has it mum». How did Kai's mum check? | She asked him to name three | She rang every parent in the class | She bought it to see for herself | She asked his teacher at the school gate
test: «why can't i have voice chat but i can have the game». What's the likely reason? | The game is fine but chat with strangers is a risk | Voice chat costs a lot extra every month | Voice chat is only for adults, by law, in every country | The game breaks if you chat
test: «noah's mum said NOT TILL 16. like, carved in stone». What's Noah's wisest move? | Accept it and stop asking | Keep asking her every single day | Play it round at a mate's house | Download the demo secretly
test: «my mum says yes, my dad says no, who wins». What usually happens? | Parents talk and agree on a rule together | Whoever shouts loudest | The child decides | The game decides
test: «grown-ups play 18 games, so unfair». Best answer? | Ratings protect younger minds, like film ratings | Grown-ups just want the best games for themselves | Ratings are made up by shops to sell more games | It's because grown-ups pay
test: «just get the demo, demos don't count». What happened to Ollie? | His mum got an email and he lost the console for sneaking | Nobody noticed, so he carried on playing it every night | Demos are hidden from parents | His mum let him keep it because demos are free to download
test: «shop wouldn't sell my brother an 18 game, he's 15». Why? | It's against the law in the UK to sell it to him | The shop didn't like the look of him | He forgot his money and his bank card at home | 18 games are only for collectors
test: «theo's parents play the first hour with him, so cringe». What's the point of it? | They see what the game's like and can talk about it | They want to beat him and show off to his mates | They are bored on weekday evenings | They want the console for themselves
test: Teacher: «What do you do if your parents say no to a game?» | Accept it calmly and maybe ask again later with reasons | Get it at a friend's house and play it there instead | Download it secretly | Refuse to do any homework until they finally agree to it
test: «leo's dad lets him play if he stops when he feels upset». Why is that a good rule? | It lets Leo try while staying safe | It means Leo plays forever | Leo never gets upset | It's a trick to stop him playing
exam: Identify the age classifications employed by the PEGI system. | 3, 7, 12, 16 and 18 | 5, 10, 15 and 20 | E, T and M only | 6, 9, 13 and 17
exam: Ascertain the method most likely to secure a favourable consideration of a request for a game by a child's parents. | The calm presentation of reasons accompanied by acceptance of the decision | Persistent repetition of the request, morning and evening, until acquiescence | Assertion that every peer possesses the game | Acquisition of the game without permission
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the assertion that «everyone has it» in respect of a game? | It is seldom accurate and does not of itself constitute a sufficient reason | It is invariably accurate and therefore decisive in every household | It obliges parents to purchase the game | It is a legal requirement for the sale of the game
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning parental controls upon game consoles. | They may restrict games by age classification and notify parents of downloads | They are incapable of restricting any game, whatever its age classification may be | They are available solely to the manufacturers | They permit children to bypass age classifications
